ve also learned from their experiences  in 2015. back then they were still  working on ways how to teach and  integrate child refugees who couldn't  speak a single word of german and on  such a large scale  so they started so-called welcoming  classes or welcome classes here children  who had just arrived in the country  would initially be given german lessons  and taught how the education system  works then they were integrated into  regular classes with other german  children their own age  several schools in berlin are already  offering welcome classes for ukrainian  children like here in berlin's wedding  district  i really like this school because  the system of education in ukraine and  germany are completely different  we like talk more  so  usually when we are in ukraine we like  read and  write  very much now the city of berlin wants  to establish 250 welcome classes which  would accommodate over 2 700 ukrainian  children but what about children who  came to germany from other parts of the  world  [Music]  b  [Music]  another vital lesson learned from 2015  was the importance of finding adequate  housing for asylum seekers which was a  massive task when the refugee crisis hit  germany back then  many new arrivals were placed in crammed  asylum centers and some had to live like  that for years  foreign  well a group of berliners wanted to  change that by providing them with a  safe space a private room and a chance  to meet locals  so they founded an ngo that finds rooms  for refugees and shared apartments  across the country  from restaurants  fled from syria to germany in 2015.  after living in mass accommodation and  then in an apartment with other refugees  organized by the state he finally  managed to get a room in an apartment  with local berliners  that was all thanks to zam liam wilcome  which means living together welcome  he says the experience completely  changed the way he integrated into  society  foreign  [Music]  support is crucial  the goal of policy in germany and in  europe should be to avoid mass  accommodation but the concept doesn't  work for everyone and the structures  aren't yet in place to make this happen  on a large scale as a small-scale ngo  suzanne li and welcome have been  operating for seven years now they've  already successfully placed recently  arrived ukrainian refugees in local  apartments in fact there's even been an  increase in room offers since russia  invaded ukraine but some potential hosts  have shown a preference for hosting  refugees from ukraine over other  countries of origin  foreign  [Music]  [Music]  a big hurdle for refugees to find jobs  in germany in the past has been that  employers are often reluctant to  recognize overseas qualifications  although more than half a million asylum  seekers have managed to secure a job in  germany in recent years it hasn't been  an easy journey many say they had to  wait for years not being allowed to work  or move cities until all the  bureaucratic red tape was dealt with  while others say they have taken on jobs  that they are highly  to be able to work is crucial and this  of course was a big problem with the  previous arrivals of asylum seekers  because the silent procedures took a  long time  because some were rejected but remain  anyways and then you know spent years in  limbo not knowing what they could do  that is a very bad policy for not just  for them but for german society and  that's something politicians decided to  change this time around a week after  russia invaded ukraine the european  council agreed on an unprecedented step  ukrainian refugees were no longer  required to apply for asylum in the eu  instead they were automatically  recognized as refugees this meant that  they were given the right to work in a  country of refuge straight away a  privilege syrian afghan and iraqi  refugees didn't have when they entered  europe back then and still don't have  when they arrived today  an army of volunteers has also stepped  up to make the employment process for  ukrainians arriving now even smoother  one of them is marcus diekmann who  founded a job portal for ukrainian  refugees in order to connect them to  employers faster and the demand is huge  but the job portal called ukrainian job  aid also received some criticism that's  because apparently it addresses  ukrainian refugees and not all refugees  in germany and because offers like this  weren't available on the same scale in  2015.  is  and there are more signs that people are  stepping up to help refugees in need one  quarter of germans already actively  volunteered back in 2015 most of them  and associations and initiatives that  exist until today now estimates show the  number  is  you know i remember 2015 huge  mobilization in germany for many many  months and in the case of many for many  years to help syrian refugees afghans  gambians the difference is  that this is a conflict next to europe  and so the media attention is much  bigger  um everyone knows what's happening in  mariupol and in kharkiv and in here  secondly the people who are coming can  only come to the european union there is  no country in between  this is  some advocates hope that the enthusiasm  germans have shown in recent weeks for  helping ukrainian refugees will continue  and that it will translate to all  refugees no matter where they come from  their religion race gender or age let's  not forget we only have this war for  seven weeks  after seven weeks in 215  those who are skeptical were not very  loud because the real test will be in  four months when there might be two and  a half million refugees in germany a  number that we've never seen before uh  when we have a shortage of every you  know of kindergarten of schools then the  test will be  if uh german society will be it will  show the same empathy as now and i hope  it will and i think we should focus on  sustaining this  because there hasn't been  a refugee crisis like this in europe  sin